WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.130
- Creativity matters everywhere,

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:05.210
if you're running for mayor in your town

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.367
or if you're running Coca Cola.

00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:09.094
(humming boom)

00:00:09.094 --> 00:00:12.011
(waves whispering)

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.320
- Hi everyone here we are in Cannes,

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.760
and I am with the
incredible Gary Vaynerchuk

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.840
I am so excited we have
been trying to do this

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.840
for the longest longest amount of time,

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.050
and he's rocked up today in
a spurs kit which my kids

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.380
are absolutely going to love.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.150
Everybody knows everything about you,

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:30.700
you are a serial entrepreneur,

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.210
you set up that first business,

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.840
a lemonade business age seven.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.490
Now chairman of VaynerX,
the ad agency VaynerMedia

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.310
and we're actually going to
talk today about authenticity

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.340
and how brands can create real
and meaningful connections

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.570
with consumers and I'm
hoping you're gonna share

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.120
some of your top tips today.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.230
So welcome to the Facebook
space, thank you for coming.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.130
- Thanks for having me.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.080
- So let's get straight in so tell me,

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.150
we're talking about
authenticity in a brand.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:59.050
What does it mean to you
from a brand communication?

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.770
- you know it's, this is
an interesting question,

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:05.360
I have a pretty for this
world a pretty left field

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.070
point of view on this, and
my point of view is we need

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:12.170
to achieve relevance I'm
looking at 11, 12, 13 faces

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:17.160
right now and what Pepsi or
BMW or Coca Cola or Facebook

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.140
means to all these people
is actually different,

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.610
and so for me authenticity
gets very close to relevancy.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.680
Because when you're a big brand
there are so many variables

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.260
in your world, there's so many
things that you are doing.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.100
You know some people may
drink a Cola to get a burst

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.170
of energy, others it may be
a reminder of spending time

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.360
with their grandmother and
I think we're so literal

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.490
in today's brand and marketing world.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.290
We're so stuck on
sentences, and adjectives,

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.430
and we spend so much
time on subjective things

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.930
that don't matter to the
consumer on the other end.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.650
So my perspective is
much like a human being,

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.210
you and I are going to
act differently as humans

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.940
in this interview than if
we were with our family.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.540
Than if we were on a weekend in Las Vegas

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.870
with our best friends,

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.140
than if we were presenting
to 5,000 people.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.080
People are always like Gary
you're a little bit different

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.620
than when I see you on video,

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.980
I'm like yeah I'm on stage
to 7,000 people versus

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:11.250
I'm here with you there's
different versions,

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.670
and I think people struggle
in ad land to understand

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.970
things that are multidimensional,

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.890
and so for me authenticity
is actually being comfortable

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:26.890
in the 74 to 7,400 variations
of how you show up,

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:32.310
and so I think that we are,
that human beings, executives.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.240
Are limiting brands
ability to be authentic

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.090
because they want it to be so literal

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:42.090
and so safe and so PR'd and
so approved by the queen bee.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:46.040
That I think we live in a
very non-authentic world

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.430
and I think the reason
humans continue to scale

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.920
in popularity is they're a focus group

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.920
of the audience, they're
an executive group of one.

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:59.650
And I think one of the
reason real celebrities

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:03.260
are struggling and have lost
share is they do have PR people

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.200
they do have big deals with movie studios

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.590
that they're scared of breaking,

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:09.950
and so I think anyone
that can actually show

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:12.930
all the versions of themselves will win,

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.460
and I think right now
the creative industry

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.770
a isn't set up to make enough creative.

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:21.270
B has way to much b to b DNA
in it to actually show up

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.930
authentic, and this is not schizophrenia,

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.700
this is not throw up against
the wall and see what sticks.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.860
This is not being scattered,
this is being true,

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:35.860
and so I think authenticity
has a far more at its end,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.410
there's always something
that delivers at the end.

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:41.750
A piece of content or creative

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:45.180
and I think it's being far
more wide than it's been

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.050
over the last 80 years.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.090
- So who is doing it well,
which brands would you point to,

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:50.003
go nailed it.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.062
- One of the things that
I don't do very well

00:03:54.062 --> 00:03:55.470
is pay attention to what
other people are doing.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.830
So this is always a tough question for me.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.580
There is something that's,
you know I thought Wendy's

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.400
did a really good job
over the last couple years

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:05.240
of engaging at scale
which then lead to a voice

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:06.840
that gave them a lot of freedom.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.010
But the reality is I know what's going on

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:13.570
in the Vayner world obviously,
I know what's going on

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:17.640
in the broad sense but I
spend almost no time auditing

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:18.640
what other brands are doing,

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.440
and plus if we're talking
from a brand standpoint,

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.130
well has to be reflected
in business results.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.800
There's a ton of people that
are going to win awards today

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.160
that business is declining dramatically

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.900
and I struggle with that.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.510
Because the point of that
creativity is to drive

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.330
a business result in this context.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.970
Or get people to vote,
or inspire a movement

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:43.150
or cure a disease, what's
the point of output

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.140
if you're not getting the end result?

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.080
You know I don't know whose doing it well,

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.700
I would say whoever's
doing it well is definitely

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.300
has business results,

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.733
and definitely has a breadth of execution.

00:04:56.831 --> 00:04:59.010
- Okay so you've been with businesses

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:00.870
and you've taken businesses from you know,

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:04.270
very small to very big do you
think there's different stages

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.620
where authenticity and
creativity matters more?

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.340
- No I think it always
matters, as a matter of fact

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.330
right now watching everybody pay attention

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:14.293
to all the sound right
behind us, and to me,

00:05:14.293 --> 00:05:16.220
and this is not a joke this is very meta.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.530
I have so much empathy especially as DRock

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:20.977
and producers in my world
have come into my world.

00:05:20.977 --> 00:05:23.510
The amount of empathy
I have for people that

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:25.610
are incredible at their skill set

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:28.060
and what they think about
is so important to me.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.270
At the same token I genuinely
live in a world where

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:32.900
I think that the person
that's consuming this

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.481
on the other side kind
of enjoys the helicopter

00:05:35.481 --> 00:05:40.100
or the boat or the
microphone because it's real,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.717
and it's not that my
point of view is right

00:05:42.717 --> 00:05:45.350
or production companies points of views

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:48.570
are right or wrong I just think that I'm

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.240
fascinated by this notion

00:05:51.145 --> 00:05:52.924
and we're living in the
meta of it right now.

00:05:52.924 --> 00:05:54.145
First of all to answer
your question directly

00:05:54.145 --> 00:05:55.310
because I wanted to
clearly make that point.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:57.080
Creativity matters everywhere,

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:00.160
if you're running for mayor in your town

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.160
or if you're running Coca Cola

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.730
and I think if you look
at what's happening.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.920
Look there's a lot of
politicians I desperately

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:10.581
don't agree with but in their authenticity

00:06:10.581 --> 00:06:13.590
of being crappy they get an audience,

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:16.770
and I think we have to learn
from that across the board.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.310
- Now earlier this year you
set up another new business,

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:22.710
The Sasha Group all focused
on small businesses,

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.760
now what one piece of
advice would you give

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.410
to a small business out
there that's looking

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:29.520
to find its voice or its personality?

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.080
- That's a great question,
lean into your founder

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:35.230
whether she or he is
introverted or extroverted.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:37.490
Because they either are extroverted

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.300
and charismatic enough
to drive your business

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.096
and you're a small company
and you need something.

00:06:41.096 --> 00:06:43.240
Or they're introverted
buy they actually know

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.710
their craft very well and
thought they may not be

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.630
the most dynamic on film
they may be incredible

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:52.690
in podcast form educating
people how to do refrigeration

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:54.740
or how to sell tires
or whatever it may be.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:58.560
So lean into your founder and
make as much creative output

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.460
as you can for the platforms
that are dominating

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:02.740
the mobile device.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:05.110
The Facebook and Instagrams,
the Snapchats and LinkedIns

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:09.050
the YouTubes of the world is
where the world is now playing,

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.320
and making content in written, audio,

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:14.950
or video form for those platforms
is imperative for an SmB.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:16.920
We were getting tens of
thousands of inquiries

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:20.660
at VaynerMedia from SmBs
and we couldn't take them on

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.020
because we're too expensive for them.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:24.410
Because the business I built
is to service big brands.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.660
But I came from SmB and I almost felt,

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.240
almost like a hypocrite slash you know

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:32.610
the reason I named it
directly after my dad Sasha

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:35.500
is 'cause that's my roots
and I thought we were ready

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:37.820
to help that world, and it's been very.

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:40.900
I would tell you honestly,
it's been far more fulfilling

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:42.310
helping these small companies,

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:45.810
I mean there's a nail business in Toronto

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:47.750
that was in deep, deep, deep trouble.

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.619
Because their main
distributor was about to,

00:07:49.619 --> 00:07:51.510
they thought they might cancel them

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:53.210
and sure enough a year later they did.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:55.970
But we helped them build a
direct to consumer business

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:57.160
through leaning in to their founder,

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:00.110
I mean I give advice that
actually I watch happen.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:03.730
We leaned into the founder,
we made a ton of Instagram,

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:07.830
Facebook, Twitter content and
the business is up three x

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.680
in revenue, you have to
understand I lived it.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:11.930
Taking my dad's business
from three mil, four million

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:15.130
to 60 million do you know
how life changing that is

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:15.963
for a business?

00:08:15.963 --> 00:08:18.470
I bought my car at a
garage sale for 500 bucks.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:22.790
My brother got a Lexus
it changes when you build

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:25.400
a business like that,
so you know we're doing

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.640
incredible work with Chase and Budweiser

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:30.760
and all this great stuff, but
when you're helping an SmB

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:32.190
and look let's call it what it is.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:34.999
Being at the Facebook
pavilion, my point of view

00:08:34.999 --> 00:08:37.430
from the outside, the small
and medium sized businesses

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:41.420
of the world have figured
out Facebook far far more

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:42.510
than the biggest brands in the world.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:46.210
Because the biggest brands in
the world deal with audacity

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:49.900
and fake and SmBs can
only live in the truth

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:52.490
because if they live in fake
they go out of business.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:54.990
- Okay so any favorite
you've mentioned a few,

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:57.890
any favorite platforms or
tools that are really good

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:00.640
for helping these businesses
to build their connections?

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:05.640
Podcast, YouTube, Facebook,
Twitter, Instagram, Snapchat,

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.170
LinkedIn has exploded
in the last six months,

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:12.240
LinkedIn acts like Facebook
did six, seven years ago.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.760
Organic reach on LinkedIn is
shocking, so there's that.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:20.050
Instagram story ads is
the most under priced ad

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:23.700
in the world, buying
Instagram swipe up story ads

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:25.810
that take you exactly to
where we want you to go,

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:28.450
for two to three or four dollars CPMs

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:30.860
and they're actually being seen?

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:33.720
Unheard of so those are some of the things

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:35.083
that I'm excited about.

00:09:35.083 --> 00:09:35.916
- Okay and what about your own channels,

00:09:35.916 --> 00:09:37.517
your own podcasts--
- I'm dominating, yeah.

00:09:37.517 --> 00:09:39.070
It's going great.
- You're own social.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:40.310
How are you learning to express,

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:42.060
not that I think you have
any troubles with this

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.870
but how do you think you're
learning, or tips that

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:46.600
you can give about how you
express yourself personally

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:48.990
and what you're able therefore to transfer

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.770
into the brands which
you can communicate for?

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:55.000
- Look the single thing I'm
most proud of sitting at Cannes

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:00.390
is there's not a human here,
not one that makes more

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:03.090
creative output than I do on a daily basis

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:05.730
and collective period,
that is an audacious

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:08.761
but factual statement, there
is not one person here.

00:10:08.761 --> 00:10:12.340
That makes more content than I do

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:15.530
and I'm a CEO of a
company, not a creative,

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:18.450
and in that you can
imagine a world where me

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:21.990
and my team are producing 40
to 100 pieces of content a day

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.650
across all these platforms,
there's a practitionership

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:28.450
that allows me, I mean
the amount of mistakes

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:30.140
you know it's funny I have DRock here

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:32.260
'cause he's on the text
chain with me and my team.

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:35.283
We change our mind seven times a month,

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:40.000
I constantly shit on my
own self once a week,

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.626
I emphatically on Monday
say all about this

00:10:42.626 --> 00:10:45.780
and literally next Tuesday
go if we every do that again

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:47.960
we're finished I mean this is real life,

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:49.750
this is the truth--
- you're testing

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:51.670
and learning with yourself.

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:54.070
- At a scale that is unseen right now,

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:56.500
and should be replicated by everyone.

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:58.900
Which gives me much better
insights to what the consumer

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:01.650
is reacting to, and then
obviously I'm a human

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:05.160
with a certain kind of flavor
so that doesn't math to Doug

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.910
the same exact way so there's
macro things that we see

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:09.930
that we deploy and then
we see how it plays out,

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:12.680
it's never literal from me to I'm a human,

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:14.650
I'm a 43 year old man
there's a lot of things

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:15.680
that don't navigate.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.360
But there's a lot of truths
that we navigate that we test

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:20.130
and learn there and that we scale,

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:23.080
so it is the ultimate test lab.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:25.430
- Okay so you, a few
bold statements there.

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:28.120
But I can say you are the
only person in all the years,

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:29.200
and I've interviewed a lot of people.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:31.430
That has ever walked in
with your own microphone.

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:33.690
'Cause you're literally
just blogging and recording

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:35.160
and observing the whole time.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:39.540
- And listen when I hear that
sentence my brain goes to

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:43.404
charlatan, self-promoter,
audacious I mean that,

00:11:43.404 --> 00:11:45.690
and I'm saying that
because I want the audience

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:48.020
to understand when you're
innovating you're gonna

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:50.350
be misunderstood, when we decided three

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:52.040
and a half years ago to film everything.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:55.820
I knew that that wasn't gonna
